    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1131">

                <pb facs="0814" n="698" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg698"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.6363/110.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Rear Admiral Sir C.
                            Cradock to the British Ambassador at
                            Washington.</hi><anchor n="96" xml:id="d1131-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Vera Cruz</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-05-07">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 7, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>From the conditions known to me and judging by my own experience I think it would
                    be rash if American citizens relied too implicitly on Mexican assurances and
                    returned now to the oil-fields. Their return might be misunderstood, and a
                    situation might arise which would imperil all foreign interests.</p>
                <closer>[Unsigned.]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1131-f1" n="96" type="source" xml:id="d1131-f1-content">
                <p>Handed to the Department of State by the British Ambassador May 7, 1914.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
